<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Adud al-Dawla</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/936">936</a>–<a href="/years/983">983</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyid dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Adud al-Dawla was the greatest ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyid dynasty</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a> from 949, he took over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> in 978, defeated his rivals and made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamdanid-dynasty">Hamdanids</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> and other neighbours submit, uniting most Buyid lands under his rule. He built hospitals, dams and libraries and took the title shahanshah.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/adud-al-dawla.jpg" alt="Adud al-Dawla" width="320" height="384" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Smithsonian · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Adud_al-Dawla_medallion.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Fanna Khusraw, later known by his honorific Adud al-Dawla, meaning pillar of the dynasty, was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> on 24 September 936, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rukn-al-dawla">Rukn al-Dawla</a> and nephew of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imad-al-dawla">Imad al-Dawla</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muizz-al-dawla">Mu'izz al-Dawla</a>. His uncle Imad al-Dawla, who had no heir, chose him as successor, and when Imad al-Dawla died in December 949 he became ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a> at the age of thirteen. Daylamite officers rebelled against him, and Rukn al-Dawla defeated them and put him on the throne at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a>. He asked the Abbasid caliph for the title Taj al-Dawla, crown of the state, but Mu'izz al-Dawla objected that it implied superiority over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a>, so the title Adud al-Dawla was chosen instead.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Adud al-Dawla annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerman">Kerman</a> in 967 and conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a> between 966 and 974, taking its port <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sohar">Sohar</a>, which secured Buyid influence over the trade routes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rukn-al-dawla">Rukn al-Dawla</a> died on 16 September 976, his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/izz-al-dawla">Izz al-Dawla</a>, the emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, stopped recognising him and allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamdanid-dynasty">Hamdanids</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a>. Adud al-Dawla defeated him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahvaz">Ahvaz</a> on 1 July 977, and on 29 May 978 he defeated him again near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarra">Samarra</a>; Izz al-Dawla was captured and executed. Adud al-Dawla then took Mosul and became sole ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyid dynasty</a>, taking the ancient Iranian title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahanshah">Shahanshah</a>, King of Kings.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> he banned public demonstrations and polemics to end sectarian violence, and he built the al-'Adudi Hospital. He also built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravanserai">caravanserais</a> and dams, among them one between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istakhr">Istakhr</a> that irrigated some 300 villages. In 982 his envoy Abu Ishaq ibn Shahram concluded a ten-year peace treaty with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Adud al-Dawla died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> on 26 March 983 and was buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najaf">Najaf</a>. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samsam-al-dawla">Abu Kalijar Marzuban</a> kept the death secret at first to secure his succession and then took the title Samsam al-Dawla, but another son, Shirdil Abu'l-Fawaris, challenged him, and civil war followed.</p><p>He kept the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> but showed more interest than earlier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a> in the pre-Islamic culture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. With the Zoroastrian chief priest Marasfand he visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persepolis">Persepolis</a>, left an inscription there and claimed descent from the Sasanian king Bahram V Gor. He still preferred Arabic authors, wrote Arabic verse and studied science in Arabic. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> he patronised an observatory where Azophi worked, and he did not follow a uniformly Shiite religious policy.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “'Adud al-Dawla” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The astronomer Abd al-Rahman al-Sufi dedicated his Book of Fixed Stars to <b>Adud al-Dawla</b>; it contains one of the earliest recorded descriptions of the Andromeda Galaxy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/buyid-dynasty">Buyid dynasty · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>In 978 <b>Adud al-Dawla</b> of the Buyids expelled the Hamdanids from Mosul; a brief return ended around 990, when the Uqaylid dynasty took over.</blockquote><a href="/state/hamdanid-emirate-of-mosul">Hamdanid emirate of Mosul</a></li><li><blockquote>In 968 <b>Adud al-Dawla</b> of the Buyids conquered Kerman, ending the dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/state/ilyasid-dynasty">Ilyasid dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=32.65,51.68&amp;year=966" aria-label="Isfahan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5876 977 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Adud al-Dawla"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6326 1247h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6326 1247h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=32.65,51.68&amp;year=966">What was here in 966 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
